Article 2 For the purposes of this Law, "intangible cultural heritage" means various forms of traditional cultural expressions that have been passed down from generation to generation by people of all ethnic groups and are regarded as part of their cultural heritage, as well as the physical objects and sites related to such traditional cultural expressions. It includes:
(1) traditional oral literature and the language serving as its carrier;
(2) traditional fine arts, calligraphy, music, dance, drama, folk art and acrobatics;
(3) traditional skills, medicine and calendar;
(4) traditional etiquette, festivals and other folk customs;
(5) traditional sports and entertainment;
(6) other forms of intangible cultural heritage.
Where the physical objects and sites that are part of intangible cultural heritage constitute cultural relics, the relevant provisions of the Law of the People's Republic of China on the Protection of Cultural Relics shall apply.

Article 19 The people's government of a province, autonomous region, or municipality directly under the Central Government may recommend to the competent department of culture under the State Council items from its local list of representative items of intangible cultural heritage for inclusion in the national list of representative items of intangible cultural heritage. When making a recommendation, the following materials shall be submitted:
(1) an introduction to the item, including its name, history, current status, and value;
(2) an introduction to the inheritance of the item, including the scope of inheritance, the genealogical tree of inheritance, the skill level of the inheritors, and the social impact of the inheritance activities;
(3) protection requirements, including the objectives to be achieved by protection and the measures, steps, and management systems to be taken;
(4) audio-visual materials and other materials that help to explain the item.

Article 29 The competent department of culture under the State Council and the competent departments of culture under the people's governments of provinces, autonomous regions, and municipalities directly under the Central Government may identify representative bearers for the representative items of intangible cultural heritage approved and promulgated by the people's governments at the corresponding level.
A representative bearer of a representative item of intangible cultural heritage shall meet the following conditions:
(1) having a thorough command of the intangible cultural heritage item that he or she inherits;
(2) being representative within a specific field and having a relatively large influence within a certain area;
(3) actively carrying out inheritance activities.
The identification of a representative bearer of a representative item of intangible cultural heritage shall be carried out by reference to the provisions of this Law on the evaluation of representative items of intangible cultural heritage, and the list of the identified representative bearers shall be made public.

Article 30 The competent departments of culture of the people's governments at or above the county level shall, as needed, take the following measures to support the representative bearers of representative items of intangible cultural heritage in carrying out inheritance and dissemination activities:
(1) providing necessary inheritance sites;
(2) providing necessary funds to support them in carrying out activities such as teaching apprentices, transmitting skills, and exchanging;
(3) supporting them in participating in public welfare activities;
(4) other measures to support them in carrying out inheritance and dissemination activities.

Article 31 A representative bearer of a representative item of intangible cultural heritage shall perform the following obligations:
(1) carrying out inheritance activities and cultivating successors;
(2) properly preserving the relevant physical objects and materials;
(3) cooperating with the competent department of culture and other relevant departments in conducting investigations of intangible cultural heritage;
(4) participating in public welfare publicity of intangible cultural heritage.
Where a representative bearer of a representative item of intangible cultural heritage fails without justifiable reasons to perform the obligations specified in the preceding paragraph, the competent department of culture may revoke his or her status as a representative bearer and re-identify a representative bearer for the item; where the bearer loses the ability to inherit, the competent department of culture may re-identify a representative bearer for the item.
